><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Reductions in some CMJ performance (JH, mean and peak&#xD;power) are likely after westbound trans-meridian travel;&#xD;however, this may be affected by training fatigue before&#xD;travel. Training loads in the weeks leading into long-haul&#xD;travel should be appropriately periodized to minimizeNMF,&#xD;which can be further exacerbated by the effects of long-haul&#xD;travel. Where possible, it is important to plan travel to arrive&#xD;at the destination with enough time to adequately adjust the&#xD;circadian rhythm to the new destination and recover before&#xD;restarting training or beginning of competition. This time is&#xD;likely to vary dependent on the number of time zones crossed&#xD;and direction of travel. Athletes would likely benefit from&#xD;recovery strategies after long-haul travel to aid in their adjustment&#xD;to the new time zone as quickly as possible, these&#xD;may include remaining mobile, completing a sport-specific&#xD;“light” training session and seeking sunlight during waking&#xD;times while ensuring adequate hydration before, during, and&#xD;after travel.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="3" rect="602.986023,302.309998,786.986023,394.309998"
/></highlight
